David Damerell wrote:
> The assertion that AD&D 1e's random dungeon is the first roguelike is a
> bit dubious. Obviously what constitutes a roguelike is a bit of a grey
> area, but one of what seems like the most important characteristics to me
> is that the movement and tactical combat interfaces are unified. Clearly a
> DM could use the random dungeon rules in a way that encouraged
> simultaneous exploration and formalised movement on the map in combat, but
> generally they didn't.

The assertion, admittedly, was a bit overboard, but my own take on what
makes a Roguelike a roguelike is random items and item identification,
both of which are in stock first edition AD&D in one form or another.
The ideas are actually a bit condensed and abstractified in Rogue and
its descendents, with their item description system.

On movement and tactical combat interfaces: I've thought a bit about
this.  I think those are important to a roguelike game only so far as
they aid the item identification and usage game, which they do to a
surprising extent.  It can certainly make a roguelike game more
interesting, sure, but it also would allow a number of things that are
obviously not roguelikes, or are at least absent those features that
make them truly interesting, like Gauntlet, into the field.

But in any case, if you were to take it that far then Dungeons &
Dragons can have a unified exploration and combat game too, depending
on how insane your DM is.

